# corpus.lock is generated. A hand-merged hash manifest can agree with neither
# side of the merge, so conflicts are resolved by regenerating:
#
#     bun run corpus:lock
#
# `-merge` stops git from attempting a line-wise textual merge and producing a
# file that looks plausible and hashes to nothing -- a conflict here must be
# resolved by regenerating, not by picking a side. `-text` leaves the
# generator's own line endings (LF) alone rather than letting git's normal
# conversion touch them. Neither attribute hides the file from diffs: unlike
# `binary` (which implies both plus `-diff`), this still shows a corpus change
# in the diff, which is the whole reason the lockfile exists.
corpus.lock -merge -text
